  "articleBody": "It's a question we hear quite often: 'Can I put marble tile directly over my existing wood floor?' And while it might seem like a straightforward solution, the answer is generally no, and here's why. Wood floors, especially older ones, tend to have a bit of flex or movement. Marble, on the other hand, is a natural stone that's incredibly rigid. When you put a rigid material like marble over something flexible like wood, that movement can cause real problems down the line. We're talking cracks in your beautiful new marble tiles or even grout lines. This isn't just about aesthetics; it compromises the integrity of your installation. For a marble installation to last and look its best, it absolutely needs a stable, properly prepared substrate. This usually means a solid backer board and a very level surface. If you're dreaming of marble in a space with a wood subfloor, it's definitely possible, but it requires the right preparation to ensure a long-lasting, crack-free result. That's where a professional really makes a difference.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}